    {
      "__v": 0,
      "_id": "66a627fc6122d647670ddba0",
      "aiGenerated": false,
      "author": {
        "_id": "60ac2099dee27167fa4ff7f5",
        "alias": "Green Hamster",
        "aliasID": "65c384dfed6eefce4cdbf582",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": "https://canny-assets.io/images/3b3cf8ed5adb45a991520ee8968d7662.png",
        "created": "2021-05-24T21:54:33.380Z",
        "name": "Megacrafter127",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "5ed571497ee72a2434704a15",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2024-07-28T11:14:04.321Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "631be9ba1672ef44b9392793",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Probably late to the party, but for some niche use-cases it might be necessary to provide a custom space for the constraint evaluation.\n\nSo I suggest adding the option for specifying transforms the constraint should evaluate relative to.\n\nOne transform for each source, that it is evaluated relative to, and one transform to apply the changes relative to.\n\nAs an example use case:\n\nA ghost hand that you can puppet.\n\nJust using local constraints, constrained lower and upper arm bones would be needed to move the hand relative to your shoulder.\n\nHowever, as there will not be an upper or lower arm, this just worsens the performance for no gain.\n\nIf you could set what transforms sources and the target are evaluated relative to, only one constraint for the hand position would be needed, that is evaluated relative to the shoulder bone, shortening the required chain length.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-09-10T01:34:50.349Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "To my knowledge, we have many constraints available to us via the default unity constraints. We can also use some extended scripting when it comes to Final IK.\nI’ve used much of this when making quadrupeds, characters with two heads, and extra arms. However, all of these systems are calculated in world space only.\nFor Avatars, I am not aware of any way to work with these constraints in local space.\nThis would be very helpful when trying to map the rotation of your arm or fingers to puppet something not directly connected to your arm or hand. Right now I’m thinking of controlling wings or ears with fingers. This level of puppeteering is significantly better than using the menus or gestures, but we don’t yet have good access to it.\nIn my own experience, I specifically ran into this issue when trying to directly control the back legs of a quadruped with my real legs. When facing straight ahead, I could puppet them just fine, but once I curved the spine, the feet would remain pointed forwards.\nI can’t imagine that this functionality would be too difficult to create. Most other unity games just copy the rotation values from one object to another and they are done… but obviously there needs to be some safety nets in place for cycle checks and other errors that might happen.\nI did my best to try and find if a similar topic already posted, there may be one, but I couldn’t find it.\nThis post is partly a “What work around are there?” As well as a plea to add this functionality to the SDK.",
